  kaidoku-shokunin/corpus/QUALITY.md-333-- **Row 194 (self-verification is mandatory on every exit).** An instrument that publishes a green verdict states that the verdict exists only through a verified receipt: the receipt is written, re-read and verified on every exit path, and a run that skips the verification has published nothing that can be graded. Two obligations follow. First, the receipt path is mandatory, not optional: --json-out is mandatory on the instrument's public command, and a run invoked without it refuses before scoring rather than scoring green and skipping the verification, so there is no exit on which a green verdict is unreceipted. Second, the verification may not be skipped and still counted: a run whose self-verification is skippable produces a green verdict that no receipt stands behind, which is the same unbound-receipt class the skip creates — the bound fields, the frozen label, the measured revision and the pinned bytes are checked by the verification, so skipping it leaves every one of them unbound at once. Round-95 M95-g is the defect this row states: the frozen round-95 counter skipped self-verification whenever it was run without `--json-out` and exited 0 on a met-True verdict with no receipt written and no field bound, while the same round's critic showed six receipt fields unbound and one forgery crashing on a bare `KeyError`; the evidence is `.ufo/waves/round-95/w1/CriticC1.md` (the no-json-out arm and the unbound-field table) and the committed before receipt `.ufo/waves/round-96/pre/c1-v5-admits-unbound-at-base.json` (accepted 5 of 6, one `KeyError` crash, nojson exit 0). The row's own limit is stated before the data: the rule binds a receipt to the world it claims and does not authenticate its provenance, so a wholly re-authored triple that describes the honest world consistently is still indistinguishable at the byte level.
  kaidoku-shokunin/corpus/QUALITY.md-334-
  kaidoku-shokunin/corpus/QUALITY.md:335:- **Row 195 (a receipt's pins name the bytes at the revision it claims).** A receipt that names a revision and carries the digests of its counter, verdict, repair and imported instruments binds each digest to `git cat-file blob <revision>:<path>`, not only to the live files of the tree that ran: a receipt measured on a dirty tree under a clean revision name carries pins that differ from the revision's bytes and is refused as `byte-pin-not-bound`. A `met_target` that is absent, null or not a real boolean is refused as `met-not-typed` before any label comparison, and a counts object that carries a key outside the nine label keys is refused as `counts-extra-key` rather than scored. Round-96 M96-a and M96-f: `tools/round-96/stop-boundary-repair-battery-v6.py` exited 0 on a dirty tree (`.ufo/waves/round-97/pre/c1-dirty-v6-admits.json`, `v6_dirty_exit 0`), and its verifier accepted an absent `met_target`. The successor `tools/round-97/stop-boundary-repair-battery-v7.py` binds the pins to the revision blobs, types `met_target` and allow-lists the counts keys; its selftest (23 cases) and the frozen kill rule's forgery battery are the evidence (`.ufo/waves/round-97/l1/scores/`). The row's own limit: the binding proves which bytes the named revision contains, not that the process which wrote the receipt was not replaced after the write.
  kaidoku-shokunin/corpus/QUALITY.md-336-
  kaidoku-shokunin/corpus/QUALITY.md-337-- **Row 196 (a crash is not a kill, and a green verdict rests on a receipt the rule re-reads).** A mutant whose check raises an uncaught exception is classified as a crash, not as a semantic kill, so a rule that counts a crash as a refusal scores a broken instrument as a caught defect; the kill class is declared per mutant and the observed class must match the declared one. A kill rule that prints a green verdict re-reads its own receipt, verifies its world revision, its counts and its typed booleans, and refuses to exit 0 when that re-read fails; it refuses before scoring when `--json-out` is absent, so no green exit exists without a receipt it can be checked against. Round-96 N9 and limit 7: a `KeyError` in the receipt schema check counted as a mutant kill (`.ufo/waves/round-96/mutants.json`, kill class N9), and the round-96 rule could exit green without a receipt file. The round-97 evidence is `.ufo/waves/round-97/pre/c1-v2rule-at-base-crash.txt` (the pinned rule crashing on the round-97 table before scoring) and `verify_own_receipt` in `tools/round-97/kill-rule-v3.py`. The row's own limit: a crash-declared mutant is classified by its traceback, so a semantic bug that happens to raise an uncaught exception is also in the crash class; the rule distinguishes classes, it does not diagnose causes.
  --
